format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: inside-the-atom

objective u1-atom-scale
statement: Know that atoms are far too small to see, even with an ordinary microscope.
misconception: A good microscope can show an atom.

item u1-l1-order-sizes
type: order
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-openstax-chem-ch2

prompt:
    Put these in order from smallest to biggest.

items:
    {"id":"atom","label":"An atom","art":"atom"}
    {"id":"virus","label":"A virus","art":"virus"}
    {"id":"cell","label":"A skin cell","art":"cell"}
    {"id":"hand","label":"A hand","art":"hand"}

explanation:
    An atom is the smallest. A virus is bigger, a skin cell is bigger still, and a hand is the biggest.

item u1-l1-tom-microscope
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-openstax-chem-ch2

statement:
    Atoms are too small to be seen with an ordinary microscope.

answer: true

explanation:
    True. An ordinary microscope uses light, and light waves are too big to show something as small as an atom. Special instruments can, but a classroom microscope cannot.

item u1-l1-tom-see-atoms
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-openstax-chem-ch2

statement:
    With a good ordinary microscope you can see a single atom.

answer: myth

explanation:
    Myth. Atoms are far too small. Even the best ordinary microscope cannot show one.

item u1-x-order-cell
type: choice
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-openstax-chem-ch2

prompt:
    Which of these is the smallest?

options:
    {"id":"atom","label":"An atom","art":"atom"}
    {"id":"virus","label":"A virus","note":"A virus is much bigger than an atom.","art":"virus"}
    {"id":"cell","label":"A skin cell","note":"A skin cell is far bigger than an atom.","art":"cell"}
    {"id":"hand","label":"A hand","note":"A hand is the biggest of these.","art":"hand"}

answer: atom

explanation:
    An atom is far smaller than a virus, a cell or a hand.

item u1-x-see-atom
type: choice
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-openstax-chem-ch2

prompt:
    Why can't you see atoms with an ordinary microscope?

options:
    {"id":"small","label":"They are far too small"}
    {"id":"few","label":"There are not enough of them","note":"There are enormous numbers of atoms in everything."}
    {"id":"energy","label":"They are made of energy","note":"Atoms are matter, not energy."}

answer: small

explanation:
    Atoms are far too small for an ordinary microscope to show.
